tion to this problem which is  perfect for the desktop imac  owc's new thunderbolt hub is able to  take just one  thunderbolt port on the imac and turn it  into one regular  usb 3.2 port and three yes i said three  full speed thunderbolt ports so if you  go for the base model imac for thirteen  hundred dollars and buy this a hundred  and fifty dollar hub  you're gonna end up with a total of four  available thunderbolt ports  and an extra usb type-a port which you  can't get on the higher-end imac models  no matter what price you pay the only  downside is that you don't get those  other upgrades like the ethernet power  supply the touch id magic keyboard or  the extra gpu core  but since the hub is only 150 dollars  you can spend that left over 50  on any of those upgrades and you'll have  four full speed thunderbill ports  or you can actually put that 50 towards  the even better  owc thunderbolt dock which packs a ton  of different ports like three  thunderbolt ports  gigabit ethernet four usb type a ports  an sd card slot and an audio jack which  i would say  is definitely worth it for those who  care about those ports so if you're  interested in either of those you can  use the links in the description below  to check them out  now getting into mistake number three if  you're planning on reselling the imac  later so you can pick up the larger imac  in the future  be very cautious about the color of the  imac that you're buying  for example everyone out there is fine  with silver since it's a classic and  neutral color  but if you're gonna be buying a yellow  imac you're instantly scratching out a  portion of the potential buyers who  think that color would look ridiculous  in a home or an office  or if you're going to be buying the pink  one you're probably going to have a much  tougher time  selling it so if you're planning on  reselling it be mindful about the color  you buy  so you might want to go with something  like blue or silver but then again  if you're keeping it long-term then buy  whatever your heart desires  but bringing us into mistake number four  if you know  for sure that you're gonna be buying the  larger imac when it comes out anyway  then it might just make sense to  completely hold off on this 24 inch imac  and wait a few months for the new one  and if you absolutely need an m1 mac  right now to get you through those few  months  then i honestly would recommend getting  the m1 macbook air  since it's currently on sale for only  900 brand new on amazon  and that model is gonna be much easier  to resell than the 24 inch imac since  it's portable and more popular  getting into mistake number five don't  think that you absolutely must go for 16  gigabytes of ram because  everybody's saying that eight gigabytes  is not enough that's simply  not true because we've ran extensive  testing on our eight gigabyte m1 macbook  air  and it performed impressively well  compared to the 16 gigabyte m1 macbook  pro  definitely not being a huge bottleneck  and although eight gigabytes of ram is  definitely not 16  apple's unified memory is incredibly  efficient so you can get much more out  of eight gigabytes of m1 memory  compared to eight gigabytes of ram on an  intel mac like we showed off in one of  our tests  we even proved that apple's unified  memory is better by completely maxing  out the ram on both an m1 macbook pro  and a windows based laptop and then  showing how the m1  barely slowed down in terms of  performance compared to the other laptop  with traditional ram so i'd say that for  most people  eight gigabytes is going to be totally  fine and if you're worried about the  whole  ssd swapping issues that people claim  the m1 max have  we actually did a full dedicated video  on that  and found that the issues were very rare  and likely due to software bugs with  rosetta 2  which may have already been dealt with  so don't worry about getting  only 8 gigabytes of ram now on the flip  side we have mistake number six  which is thinking that you can upgrade  the ram yourself after you buy it  which you can actually do on the 27 inch  intel imax but that is not the case with  apple silicon max so it is very  important that you buy  the amount of ram that you think you'll  need right away  this is going to be the case mainly for  people who are using or planning to use  high-end productivity apps like xcode  logic and especially photo editing since  those tasks can really eat up a lot of  ram  and if you're thinking of potentially  running virtual machines in the future  like running windows 10 using parallels  as we showed off with our m1 macbook pro  then getting 16 gigabytes of ram is a  really good idea  since you have to dedicate your ram to  that virtual machine but once again  if you're somebody who doesn't do any of  this high-end productivity work  eight gigabytes should be just fine and  you'll be able to spend that extra 200  on something else say extra ssd storage  and that brings us to mistake number  seven you need to be wise about the  amount of storage that you buy  because like ram you cannot upgrade it  yourself  so some people are going to be buying  the base model with only 250 gigabytes  of storage  and then suffering a few years down the  road when they're completely out of  storage  and they can't figure out what to keep  or what to delete but then on the flip  side  somebody's gonna go out and buy the two  terabyte ssd storage model for an  extra 800 and then a couple years later  when they're ready to resell it and buy  something else  they will realize that you won't get  anywhere near that extra 800  for the ssd when they didn't even get  close  to using even one terabyte so the point  that i'm trying to make is be wise about  your storage and the best way to do that  is to look at how much storage you're  using on your current computer  and then order based off of that and  then finally for mistake number eight  consider buying an external storage  drive because you can save  a ton of money and with the imac you  have a desktop setup and not a macbook  so internal storage isn't as big of a  deal for example  this sabrint rocket extreme ssd that we  recommend with 2 terabytes of storage  is only 360 on amazon compared to paying  apple a massive  800 for their two terabytes and since  this is a thunderbolt drive  you're gonna get incredibly fast  transfer speeds similar to what you get  with the imac's internal storage  and on top of that if you end up  reselling your imac sooner than you  initially thought  you get to keep the ssd drive and reuse  it for your next computer  so it's really a great solution and now  with all those mistakes out of the way  let's finally get into the buyer's guide  if you're buying the 24 inch imac as a  reliable computer for casual or family  use  mostly relying on web-based apps or for  internet or for business  schooling stuff like that then i would  honestly just go with the base 1300  model you definitely don't need 16  gigabytes of ram and you don't need to  upgrade to the a core gpu model  and then if you really want the touch id  magic keyboard you can upgrade to that  separately  and potentially even upgrade to a 512  gigabyte ssd  if you have a big family who will all be  using it now if you want a reliable  computer but you're also going to be  using it for some light productivity  work  like some basic video or photo editing  then i would argue that sticking with  eight gigabytes of ram should be just  fine  but you'll definitely want at least 512  gigabytes of ssd  and i don't think that the 7 core gpu on  the base model is a big deal at all  but if you're taking your work very  seriously with professional programming  music production video editing photo  editing or anything else that you rely  on to make money  then i would definitely upgrade to 16  gigabytes of ram and either get the  mid-level spec with the extra usb type-c  ports or buy one of those odbc  thunderbolt hubs for the extra ports  and at that point you should at least be  buying a 512 gigabyte ssd model unless  you can rely on fast external storage  like the sabrint ssd drives and finally  if you're somebody who's thinking of  running  parallel 16 to play games under windows  10 i would definitely recommend  a large ssd for downloading games and 16  gigabytes of ram since the m1 gpu  depends on it  and at that point if you really take  gaming seriously i would honestly just  wait for the larger m1x imac  which should be coming later this year  with a whole lot more performance for  gaming  so hopefully this video helped you make  a decision and if it did consider using  our links down below to order  but if you're still confused comment  down below with your specific use case  and we can help you make a decision  thank you guys for watching it's been  max click that circle above if you guys  want to subscribe and help us reach  1 million subscribers by the end of this  year you can watch one of those videos  right over there and i'll see you in the  next video

